@meta
id: 09-18
title: Pick the motion that needs no count
chapter: 09
type: lesson
skills: op-motion, op-linewise, op-counts
par: 25
minutes: 6
vscode: three separate selections, three Deletes, one miscounted
open: refund.service.ts

@brief
Three bits of rot in one service, all of them deletions:

  1. The `// TODO drop the actor` comment on the `refund` line goes. The
     call itself, semicolon included, stays as it is.
  2. `refundLegacy` goes — the method and one of the blank lines around
     it, leaving the usual single gap.
  3. Everything below the closing brace of the class is dead since the
     rewrite. It goes too.

Every one of these can be done by counting lines or words. Do none of
them that way.

@keys
| `d$` | to the end of the line |
| `d}` | to the next blank line |
| `dG` | to the end of the file |
| `18G` | to line eighteen |

@hints
Three edits, three different extents: to the end of a line, to the end of
a block, to the end of the file. There is a motion for each, and not one
of them takes a number.
--
Work bottom-up. Deleting the tail of the file first means the line
numbers above it never move, so the second and third jumps still mean
what you worked out at the start.
--
`18G dG`, then `9G d}`, then `7G f/ d$`.

@solution
    18G dG    9G d}    7G f/ d$    :wq<Enter>

Nineteen keystrokes for three deletions of wildly different sizes, and
the only numbers involved were line numbers you could read off the gutter.

That is the judgement this chapter is really about. Almost any edit can be
expressed as a count — `d4j`, `d12w`, `3dd` — and counts are seductive
because they always work, right up until the file changes by one line and
they silently do the wrong thing. A motion that describes the *structure*
does not have that failure mode. `dG` is correct however long the tail is.
`d}` is correct however many lines the method grows to. `d$` is correct
whatever the comment says.

The habit to build: before you count, ask what the end of the thing you
are deleting actually is. End of statement? `dt;`. End of argument list?
`dt)`. End of block? `d}`. End of file? `dG`. Somewhere you can see but
not describe? `ma` and `d'a`. Counting is what is left when the answer to
that question is genuinely "four lines".

Working bottom-up is the other habit. Every edit you make above the
cursor invalidates the line numbers below it. Start at the bottom and the
numbers you noted at the beginning stay true all the way through.

@start refund.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { GatewayClient } from './gateway.client';

@injectable()
export class RefundService {
  public async refund(orderId: string, amount: number) {
    return this.gateway.refund(orderId, amount, 'AUTO'); // TODO drop the actor
  }

  public async refundLegacy(orderId: string) {
    return this.gateway.refundLegacy(orderId);
  }

  public async cancel(orderId: string) {
    return this.gateway.cancel(orderId);
  }
}

// everything below is dead since the rewrite
export function reconcileByHand() {
  return null;
}

@expected refund.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { GatewayClient } from './gateway.client';

@injectable()
export class RefundService {
  public async refund(orderId: string, amount: number) {
    return this.gateway.refund(orderId, amount, 'AUTO');
  }

  public async cancel(orderId: string) {
    return this.gateway.cancel(orderId);
  }
}
